    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many positive factors of 96 are also multiples of 12, we start by finding the prime factorization of 96.\n\nFirst, we divide 96 by the smallest prime number, which is 2:\n\\[ 96 \\div 2 = 48 \\]\n\\[ 48 \\div 2 = 24 \\]\n\\[ 24 \\div 2 = 12 \\]\n\\[ 12 \\div 2 = 6 \\]\n\\[ 6 \\div 2 = 3 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\div 3 = 1 \\]\n\nSo, the prime factorization of 96 is:\n\\[ 96 = 2^5 \\times 3^1 \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the factors of 96 that are multiples of 12. A factor of 96 that is a multiple of 12 must have at least \\(2^2 \\times 3^1\\) in its prime factorization. We can express 96 as follows:\n\\[ 96 = 2^5 \\times 3^1 \\]\n\nTo find the factors of 96 that are multiples of 12, we take the highest possible powers for the prime factors in 12 within the factorization of 96:\n- \\(2^2\\) (I can only use it once because \\(2^2\\) can be found in \\(2^2 \\times 3^1\\), which is less than the required \\(2^2\\)).\n- \\(3^1\\) (required \\(3^1\\) is present already).\n\nWe can rewrite 12 as \\(2^1 \\times 3^1\\), so the factors of 96 that are multiples of 12 are of the form \\(2^a \\times 3^b\\) where \\(a\\) can be 0, 1, or 2 (since \\(2^2\\) is already used) and \\(b\\) can be 1 (since \\(3^1\\) is required). This gives us the following possibilities:\n- \\(2^0 \\times 3^1 = 3\\)\n- \\(2^1 \\times 3^1 = 6\\)\n- \\(2^2 \\times 3^1 = 12\\)\n- \\(2^0 \\times 3^2 = 9\\)\n- \\(2^1 \\times 3^2 = 18\\)\n- \\(2^2 \\times 3^2 = 36\\)\n- \\(2^0 \\times 3^0 = 1\\) (but this is not a multiple of 12) and so on...\n\nWhen we list them, all these numbers are simply the factorials of their powers, which means all of these numbers are valid positive factors of 96 that are multiples of 12. Thus, the factors of 96 that are multiples of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 18, 24, 36, and 48. \n\nCounting them, we see there are 12 such factors. Therefore, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]",
